WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:09.200
Throughout history, our biggest leaps in technology

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:12.420
have occurred as a result 
of new materials becoming available.

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:16.040
I've shown you how it took humankind centuries 
to master iron and steel,

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:19.860
culminating in the explosion 
of growth during the industrial revolution;

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:22.580
how the accidental discovery of age hardening allowed

00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:25.580
aluminium to take humans 
into the stratosphere in droves;

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:28.940
and how silicon forms the backbone 
of this age of information.

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:33.260
But today, we're going to discover 
the next great leap in material science:

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:35.600
carbon fiber reinforced plastics.

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:39.940
Carbon fiber, and its even more futuristic brother, 
carbon nano tubes,

00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:42.380
have been touted as the next great innovation

00:00:42.380 --> 00:00:46.200
that will allow humans to build amazing structures 
that were once unimaginable.

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:51.960
Elon Musk recently revealed an enormous 
carbon fiber-reinforced plastic cryogenic fuel tank,

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:55.320
the largest fuel tank 
that has ever been created for spaceflight.

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:58.280
Building spaceships of this size is not an easy task.

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:02.260
The larger the spaceship, 
the more fuel you need to escape the Earth's gravity.

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:05.580
The more fuel you need, 
the bigger the fuel tank needs to be to hold it,

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:07.700
which just adds more mass to the rocket.

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:11.740
Carbon fiber reinforced plastics 
provided a new way to keep the weight down

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:14.940
while maintaining the strength needed 
to withstand the internal pressures.

00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:19.280
But building a structure like this 
from carbon fiber is no easy task.

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:22.660
This is the sort of material that 
if it crash-landed on Earth

00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:26.380
just 70 years ago, 
it would have been a completely alien object,

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:28.900
and we are still learning a lot about its mechanics.

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:33.780
The fact SpaceX have created a structure this size 
out of carbon fiber reinforced plastics

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:38.380
that has already been tested at two thirds
of its bursting pressure is astounding by itself,

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:42.980
but we've yet to see whether it can resist 
the blistering cold temperatures of the liquid fuels

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:45.920
without cracking and leaking, and do it repeatedly.

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:50.060
After all, the whole point of SpaceX rockets 
is to be reusable.

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:52.640
Building a cryotank out of composite materials

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:56.740
is without a doubt the most difficult part 
of building a spaceship of this size.

00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:59.080
Don't believe me? Listen to the man himself say it.

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:13.100
Nevertheless, the material will be pivotal 
in reducing the weight of our craft enough

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:17.260
to escape the gravity prison 
that has kept humans as a single-planet species.

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:20.300
Even though we are still learning a lot about the material,

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:23.840
carbon fibers by themselves 
are not as new as you may think.

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:27.760
The first carbon fibers were produced 
by Thomas Edison and Joseph Swan

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:31.400
for use as filaments 
in their new electric incandescent lamps.

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:33.740
The carbon fibers they created were produced

00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:37.180
by carbonizing plant threads like 
cotton, wood, and bamboo.

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:40.780
This material did not have the tensile strength 
of today's carbon fibers,

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:45.680
but they allowed Edison and Swan to replace 
the expensive Platinum filaments being used earlier.

00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:50.540
The carbon bamboo filaments Edison developed 
lasted up to 1200 hours,

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:54.360
and they were the norm for 10 years 
until tungsten filaments replaced them.

00:02:54.580 --> 00:02:57.880
Not much thought was put 
into carbon fiber as a structural material

00:02:57.880 --> 00:03:01.460
until the late 1950's:
when the Union Carbide Corporation,

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:04.340
a company that manufactured light bulb filaments,

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:07.120
began investigating replacements 
for tungsten filaments.

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:11.020
They started using Rayon, 
an artificial, cellulose-based material.

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:15.900
popular as a cheap alternative to silk 
and cotton as the base ingredient for carbon fibers.

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:19.840
This was a breakthrough moment for the start 
of the modern carbon fiber era

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:23.320
as a resulting materials
showed potential as a structural material.

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:25.960
Researchers all over the world took notice,

00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:29.100
and just a month later, 
the Japanese Industrial Research Institute

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:34.600
began investigating how to make their own fibers from Polyacrylonitrile or "PAN" for short.

00:03:34.600 --> 00:03:37.340
The majority of today's carbon fiber
is produced with PAN,

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:40.520
even though it is 
an expensive byproduct of oil production.

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:45.740
It yields a fiber with a much higher percentage 
of carbon than Rayon, and it's easier to manufacture.

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:49.240
Dr. Shindo created long strands of this PAN material,

00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:54.420
which he stabilized by first heating it 
to around 250 degrees for up to two hours.

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:58.740
This rearranges the chemical bonding to create a thermally stable ladder bonding.

00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:03.920
These fibers were then heated again 
to around 1,000 degrees in the absence of oxygen,

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:07.000
which expels the non carbon atoms 
in the material

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:11.400
leaving tightly-bonded carbon atoms 
arranged mostly in the direction of the fiber.

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:17.380
This material was both heat and chemical resistant. 
It had fantastic tensile strength, and didn't rust.

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:21.100
Specialized industries, like aerospace, 
immediately saw its potential

00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:23.500
to help spacecraft break the grip of Earth's gravity.

00:04:23.620 --> 00:04:28.060
And when Gay Brewer won 
the Taiheiyo Club Masters' Golf Tournament in 1972,

00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:32.760
using a carbon fiber composite shaft,
the material captured the world's attention too.

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:37.360
The sporting goods manufacturers all over the world 
began creating carbon fiber products.

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:39.920
Golf clubs, tennis rackets, and racing bikes

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:44.860
started to be made with the cheaper off-spec fibers
that the aerospace industry could not use.

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:48.240
Despite its high price, 
demand for the material kept growing,

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:51.800
and by 1980, 
1,000 metric tons were being produced a year.

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:54.840
But it was still 
a notoriously difficult material to work with,

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:58.200
and many still doubted 
its ability as a structural material.

00:04:58.460 --> 00:05:00.900
During the development of the McLaren MP4/1,

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:04.980
one skeptical engineer had said to have picked up 
a piece of carbon fiber composite

00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:07.780
just like this and easily snapped it in half,

00:05:07.960 --> 00:05:10.320
declaring that the material was too weak and brittle

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:13.500
to be used for the ambitious 
carbon fiber composite monocoque.

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:16.240
But he was breaking the material across the fibers;

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:20.620
if he had attempted to break it along the fibers, 
he would've had serious difficulty.

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:24.640
You see, carbon fiber is made 
from these tiny thin fibers.

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:29.040
This is a picture showing the size difference 
between a carbon fiber and a human hair.

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:30.860
The carbon fiber is the smaller one.

00:05:31.060 --> 00:05:34.600
These tiny fibers by themselves 
can't be used as a material,

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:39.000
we first have to bind 
these long strands of fiber together with a plastic resin.

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:43.240
Otherwise, they're just a flimsy fabric 
that can't hold any load other than tension.

00:05:43.460 --> 00:05:47.960
Here you can see a solid piece of carbon fiber 
where the fibers are surrounded by white resin,

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:52.460
but this technique causes some problems for engineers 
when designing a product with this material

00:05:52.460 --> 00:05:54.940
because the plastic is weaker than the fibers.

00:05:55.160 --> 00:05:58.840
This is a unidirectional layer of Carbon fiber 
held together with resin.

00:05:58.900 --> 00:06:01.280
If we pull it this way along the fibers,

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:06.780
it has fantastic strength because the fiber is 
in the same direction as the load, and, thus, can resist it.

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:11.420
But if we pull it this way, 
there is no continuous run of fibers to resist the force.

00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:16.760
Instead, the resin matrix gets pulled apart, 
and the fibers add very little strength to the material.

00:06:16.760 --> 00:06:20.620
To combat this, engineers will layer 
the fibers on top of each other.

00:06:20.620 --> 00:06:24.100
Here we have a zero degree layer 
on top of a 90 degree layer,

00:06:24.100 --> 00:06:27.040
so, the material properties are the same 
in those directions.

00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:29.700
But now, the 45 degree direction is weaker.

00:06:29.700 --> 00:06:33.620
We can keep adding more layers until 
we have similar stiffness in all directions.

00:06:33.860 --> 00:06:36.540
But eventually, the thickness 
of the material will become too great.

00:06:36.980 --> 00:06:38.100
We reach another problem:

00:06:38.120 --> 00:06:42.260
when we remember there is nothing 
but the resin matrix holding these layers together,

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:45.520
and sometimes that fails, 
and we get de-lamination of the layers.

00:06:45.520 --> 00:06:49.420
This is why you often see the carbon fibers 
woven into a fabric like this.

00:06:49.420 --> 00:06:52.300
But again, woven fabrics can only be so thick.

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:55.580
We also need to worry about 
the drapeability of the material,

00:06:55.580 --> 00:06:57.900
as these fabrics need to be shaped over molds,

00:06:57.900 --> 00:07:01.620
and a very thick material like this
can make that job very difficult.

00:07:01.700 --> 00:07:05.320
These issues create a whole lot of work 
for engineers working with composites.

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:09.860
But with experience, we've learned to use these unique material characteristics to our advantage.

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:14.860
The designers of the MP4/1 managed to create
such a strong monocoque with the material

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:18.500
that when the car did crash,
it silenced many of its doubters.

00:07:18.860 --> 00:07:22.600
We can tailor the fiber directions
to optimize the material properties.

00:07:22.900 --> 00:07:26.720
Here, we perform the tensile tests 
on a tiny piece of carbon fiber composite

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:29.340
with fibers only in the direction of the force,

00:07:29.380 --> 00:07:33.260
creating an incredibly rigid structure 
that barely deformed through the test

00:07:33.260 --> 00:07:36.700
and managed to hold 5.3 tonnes before breaking.

00:07:36.860 --> 00:07:39.160
But stiffness like that is not always desired.

00:07:39.380 --> 00:07:41.460
For example, in the event of a crash,

00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:45.220
you want your car to crumble and deform 
to absorb as much energy as possible.

00:07:45.220 --> 00:07:50.200
And so composite noses of F1 cars are designed 
to fragment into millions of tiny bits,

00:07:50.200 --> 00:07:54.180
and this helps absorb energy 
and decelerate the car more slowly.

00:07:54.340 --> 00:07:59.660
Pressure vessels like the Dreamliner fuselage are wrapped in layer after layer of resin-soaked carbon fiber

00:07:59.660 --> 00:08:02.500
by a robot that places it in the exact right position,

00:08:02.500 --> 00:08:05.820
making the structure perfect 
for withstanding internal pressure.

00:08:05.820 --> 00:08:09.440
One of the big problems 
when building a structure this big with composite,

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:13.580
is that they need to be placed in an autoclave 
to get the best quality material.

00:08:13.640 --> 00:08:17.660
An autoclave applies heat and pressure 
to the material to set the resin

00:08:17.660 --> 00:08:22.660
and forces the air and other voids that weaken 
the material out of the resin during the curing process.

00:08:22.660 --> 00:08:26.040
The autoclave Boeing uses 
for the Dreamliner is massive,

00:08:26.040 --> 00:08:30.000
and there isn't an autoclave in the world big enough 
to fit the tank SpaceX created.

00:08:30.380 --> 00:08:35.300
It's hard to know how they did it, but they almost certainly used an automated tape-placement robot,

00:08:35.300 --> 00:08:38.320
like the one in Boeing use 
to create their own smaller cryotanks,

00:08:38.360 --> 00:08:42.540
which can cure the resin with a laser 
as it lays the pre-preg carbon fiber tape down.

00:08:43.120 --> 00:08:45.800
But it's very hard to create 
quality parts with this method,

00:08:46.140 --> 00:08:50.000
as the pressure of the autoclave is essential for removing voids in the resin,

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:52.780
And this is such a huge concern for composite manufacturers,

00:08:52.780 --> 00:08:55.520
that parts are often scanned with ultrasound technology

00:08:55.520 --> 00:08:59.040
to check that the resin has cured 
with a tolerable amount of micro-voids.

00:08:59.180 --> 00:09:04.440
This machine sends ultrasound into the part through a jet of water, and detects the voids in the material.

00:09:04.580 --> 00:09:07.440
These voids are an even bigger 
concern for this application

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:12.400
because the biggest problem for the material is cryogenic fuel leaking through micro-cracks and voids.

00:09:12.560 --> 00:09:14.360
Looking at the outside of the tank,

00:09:14.360 --> 00:09:17.700
I would say with a fair bit of certainty
that the tank was made in two halves

00:09:17.700 --> 00:09:21.120
that could fit in an autoclave, 
and was then riveted and welded together

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:23.800
which I would imagine will be removed
for the final version

00:09:23.800 --> 00:09:25.980
as this just creates weakness in the structure,

00:09:25.980 --> 00:09:27.280
but this is all guess work.

00:09:27.540 --> 00:09:30.900
buh-buh-buh-buh-buh buh-breaking news, yeah!

00:09:32.180 --> 00:09:37.780
So, while I was making this video, pictures were posted to the SpaceX subreddit by u/Death_Cog_Unit

00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:41.100
showing the remains of the cryotank after being tested.

00:09:41.100 --> 00:09:43.520
It failed exactly where I said it was going to fail,

00:09:43.520 --> 00:09:47.720
but SpaceX have yet to make a formal announcement about the results of this new test.

00:09:48.220 --> 00:09:50.000
This does not mean failure of the project.

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:55.500
We test things to learn more, and it's fantastic that SpaceX are pushing the boundaries of what is possible.

00:09:55.500 --> 00:10:00.240
I still have so many questions about how this thing was made, which just makes it more frustrating

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:04.460
that all the questions being asked in the Q&amp;A session after the big reveal were so stupid.

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:07.260
This advancement, if we can make it work,

00:10:07.260 --> 00:10:10.900
will be the most significant leap in rocket technology we've seen in recent memory.

00:10:11.420 --> 00:10:16.740
Boeing stated that their cryotank provided 40% weight savings over traditional aluminium fuel tanks.

00:10:17.220 --> 00:10:21.720
That is going to allow us to launch larger vehicles into orbit, reach further into our solar system,

00:10:21.720 --> 00:10:27.300
and hopefully, not in the too distant future, it will help humans become a multiplanetary species.

